{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/953292-how-to-handle-time-travel","id":953292,"title":"How to handle Time travel","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-08-18T10:58:20-07:00","id":953292,"title":"How to handle Time travel","content":"I've got plans to run some sort of Mage storyline for my regular D&amp;D group. Several people have expressed interest in Mage, and I've been a fan of the franchise for quite a while (but never had a chance to play). One of the things that I'm really uncertain about how to approach is all the crazy stuff you can do with Time. How do you handle it in practise? If someone manages to spend several reach on Shifting Sands to completely undo several scenes ... how do you handle it as a group? All the other characters would forget what's happened ... but how do you decide what happened instead? Do you regularly jump back and forth, to play out different timelines? Doesn't this run the risk of taking away the agency of other players, for instance if another player did something awesome, but the Acanthus mage decides that things didn't go as well as they wanted, so jump back, change things and now the first player's awesome thing never happened? <br /> <br /> And how would you handle something really major like Corridors of Time? <br /> <br /> All of this sounds cool in theory, and I don't think I'd have any problems with chaning a turn or a few, but these major timeline alterations seem ... well, too much like Star Trek time travel. Any insights you have would be nice to read <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11756-razikale","name":"Razikale","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"30"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-18T11:22:47-07:00","id":953310,"title":"","content":"If you don't think you can handle it the first step is talk to your table about it. That said, there are people who've been managing. <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/934021-pentacle-city-continued-actual-play\">http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/m...ed-actual-play</a> This Actual Play even has an example.<br /> <br /> By the way, bringing your whole cabal back with you is just -2 Area scale if they're not too spread out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 08-18-2016, 02:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-18T11:31:24-07:00","id":953318,"title":"","content":"If you think official Time Travel rules are too much headache - you can use our fan alternative spells. They are based on two ideas:<br /> 1. Time travel is limited to turns on Time 3 and hours on Time 5.<br /> 2. Going over those limits made any Time magic on character Paradox Risking.<br /> <br /> Latest version of those rules are <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/917024-2e-hack-alternative-time-travel-magic?p=920221#post920221\">here</a> - hopes you like it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-18T11:32:51-07:00","id":953320,"title":"","content":"And as I noted in that thread, they don't actually fix the problems induced by time travel.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-19T09:31:06-07:00","id":953884,"title":"","content":"Ask your time mage to cook up a spell that will let them remember and see past through any time stuff he does. Cast it every day and unsafely release it. This way you remove the most annoying aspect of the time travel (only one player being able to remembe and interact with knowledge gained).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-22T15:20:10-07:00","id":955926,"title":"","content":"I've been running a time travel heavy chronicle, Mrmduois has shared a link to the actual play above so I hope that might help to see it in practice.<br /> <br /> There's an old joke, a small town that can't afford one lawyer can always afford two. I feel like it's similar with time magic, especially time travel. If you act like the players are the only ones in the entire world that have it then it will make a chronicle very difficult to run, however it strangely becomes easier the more common you consider time travel to be. It becomes integrated into the world and people come to expect it, plan for it and even make defenses against it. Don't do this so much that time travel becomes useless, but just make it so that it's not the answer to every problem. As an example if the Seers of the Throne steal a powerful grimoire from the Pentacle, then they're going to cas<i>t Constant Moment </i>on the grimoire so that no one can get it back using time travel.<br /> <br /> With Shifting Sands, don't forget that it's only one reach for the player to go from just bringing themselves back to targeting up to five people. This means that the acanthus can bring their entire cabal with them on time travel adventures. This always makes it easier for everyone if it's the entire cabal, or the entire cabal minus one person who is the one they're going back to save. <br /> <br /> One of our players is a Mastigos and I always make it a really easy spell for him to share the memories of the time traveler with everyone else, just because it makes the chronicle easier on everyone. Technically that should probably be a more challenging spell, but since everyone on the table has the same goal of making the game work we just all look the other way on that one. Likewise even without mind magic, I let players just say &quot;I explain everything that they missed&quot; without having to say everything in character to get everyone up to speed.<br /> <br /> Likewise I let the players discuss out of character whether to use shifting sands or not. Normally I prefer for the players to stay in character with discussions of strategy but because this affects everyone's experience of the game it's everyone's business. So for example in the latest session when Martina goes back in time on her own without the rest of the cabal, in character that was done without asking anyone. Out of character though, Martina's player asked the entire group how they felt about it and everyone agreed that they were happy to do it.<br /> <br /> An in character solution that my player's cabal has, is any time someone is a time-traveler from the future they have a special phrase &quot;the gods are angry&quot; that they say to identify that they've just come back from the future. They planned this out in advance so that they don't have to have long-winded explanations when they go back to give a warning. They can just say &quot;the gods are angry, don't open that door.&quot; <br /> <br /> We always keep all beats and XP earned in every timeline, regardless of anything else. This means that the players may end up having a groundhog day adventure like the one from my actual play, but they still get to advance and develop.<br /> <br /> That's all I can think of just at the moment. There's no denying that time travel is a difficult thing to handle in a chronicle, but it can be a lot of fun too.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6748-michael-kenner","name":"Michael Kenner","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6748_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"380"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-22T15:38:24-07:00","id":955931,"title":"","content":"Major time changes can be handled by a Time Patrol type legacy that comes from the futrue to set right what went wrong and kick the asses of mages who set it wrong back into the present week. If the ST feels generous, Then the cabal that shows up is less Time Patrol and more Time Squad, meaning that they don't really know their history well enough to enforce it correctly, so the PCs might be able to trick them into thinking a change is the way it's supposed to be.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Despite the user name I am actually bigender.<br /> <br /> My Savannah Setting for CofD can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/959455-the-young-man-in-the-cafe-s-savannah-georgia-setting-writeup\" target=\"_blank\">here</a><br /> <br /> My heroes as monster tamers rules for BtP can be found <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/975230-heroes-as-monster-tamers-rules-ideas\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3932-the-young-man-in-the-cafe","name":"The young man in the cafe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3932_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-22T17:52:22-07:00","id":956030,"title":"","content":"A legacy isn't large enough to effectively police more than 1/5 of the Mage population no matter how hard they try, and Mages would know it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-22T23:26:47-07:00","id":956178,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6748-michael-kenner\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar6748_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"6748\" data-vbnamecard=\"6748\">Michael Kenner</a>, interesting points on Time Travel. Will think on them more and comment...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-23T07:42:16-07:00","id":956313,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/953292-how-to-handle-time-travel?p=956030#post956030\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A legacy isn't large enough to effectively police more than 1/5 of the Mage population no matter how hard they try, and Mages would know it.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair they do have a lot of time on their hands. :-p<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1563-oriontyson","name":"OrionTyson","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1563_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}